# Migrating `TimedExecutor` to .NET 8

## Key Problem

`Thread.Abort()` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8. The cooperative cancellation pattern using `CancellationToken` is the replacement.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Will throw if the task doesn't complete in time
            task.Wait(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }

        // Unwrap AggregateException for single faults
        if (task.IsFaulted)
            throw task.Exception!.InnerExceptions.Count == 1
                ? task.Exception.InnerException!
                : task.Exception;

        return task.Result;
    }

    // Async overload (preferred in modern code)
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token).WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

## Usage

```csharp
var executor = new TimedExecutor();

// The work delegate must observe the token
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(ct =>
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // cooperative check
        // ... do work ...
    }
    return 42;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
```

## Migration Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Non-cooperative work** | Unlike `Thread.Abort()`, cancellation only works if the delegate checks the token. Long-running library calls that don't accept a `CancellationToken` won't be interrupted. |
| **Resource leaks** | `Thread.Abort()` could leave locks/handles in bad states silently. Cancellation is safer but the abandoned `Task` may still run briefly — always dispose resources in `finally`. |
| **`default(T)` capture** | The original code captured `result` across threads unsafely. `Task<T>` eliminates this data race. |
| **Exception semantics** | `Task.Run` wraps exceptions in `AggregateException`. The migrated code unwraps single exceptions to match original behavior. |
| **`ThreadAbortException` handlers** | Remove all `catch (ThreadAbortException)` blocks — the exception no longer exists. Replace with `catch (OperationCanceledException)`. |

## For truly uncooperative code

If you cannot modify the work delegate (e.g., calling a third-party library with no cancellation support), the only safe option is **process isolation**:

```csharp
// Run in a child process and kill it on timeout
using var process = Process.Start("WorkerApp.exe", args);
if (!process.WaitForExit(timeout))
{
    process.Kill(entireProcessTree: true);
    throw new TimeoutException("...");
}
```

There is no safe in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort()` in .NET 8.
